{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And it was after the plague, etc.</b> One needs to know why he made the beginning of a parashah in the middle of the verse. And according to what I wrote above there is a correct reason, that by this it intended to say that in the midst of the commandment of ״צָרוֹר״ etc., in the manner we explained, they rectified the calculation of idolatrous worship and inclined themselves to the divine nature, and in this ״וַיְהִי אַחֲרֵי הַמַּגֵּפָה״ — meaning, the plague that was upon Israel ceased by means of this rectification that HaShem commanded them to do. And therefore he began the parashah in the middle of the verse, to say that the statement ״אַחֲרֵי הַמַּגֵּפָה״ is connected with what is above it. And the reason he did not make it a verse on its own is that he also intended to connect it to what is below — that after the plague HaShem said etc. ״שְׂאוּ״ etc. You find yourself saying that the statement ״וַיְהִי אַחֲרֵי הַמַּגֵּפָה״ serves upward to express one matter, and downward to express another matter." "<b>Further</b>, it appears to give a reason why he began the parashah from the middle of the verse, based on the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Yalkut Shimoni Tehillim 693) who said, and this is their language: ״וַיְהִי אַחֲרֵי הַמַּגֵּפָה שְׂאוּ אֶת רֹאשׁ״ etc. — this is what the verse said: ״אִם אָמַרְתִּי מָטָה רַגְלִי״ etc. At the time Israel received the Torah, the nations of the world were jealous, etc. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to them: Bring me your book of genealogies, etc. When they came to Shittim they corrupted themselves, etc. The nations said: The crown that was in their hand has been annulled — they are equal to us. HaShem straightened them, for He struck down all who had corrupted themselves and established the rest in their purity — end quote. According to this, there is a reason for the break in the middle of the verse, to hint that the statement is also connected with what is above concerning the evils that Midian caused on account of Peor and on account of Cozbi etc. And also <b>וַיְהִי אַחֲרֵי הַמַּגֵּפָה</b> means an expression of pain, and this is what the verse alludes to in ״מָטָה רַגְלִי״ — that the nations of the world belittled them because the crown that had been in their hand was taken away. And this belittlement was not removed from Israel even when the plague ceased from them, until He counted them and every one was found recognizing his fathers by tribe — then they lifted their head. And this is His saying <b>וַיְהִי</b> as an expression of pain even after the plague, and therefore ״וַיֹּאמֶר HaShem etc. שְׂאוּ״, and He did not decide to break by making it a verse on its own and rather combined it with what is below it, to say that because of what is alluded to in the word ״וַיְהִי״ — which is the belittlement of the nations — it is that HaShem said ״שְׂאוּ״ etc." "<b>Further</b>, there is reason to give for why he began the parashah from the middle of the verse based on the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Bamidbar Rabbah 21:7), who said and this is their language: When Israel went out of Egypt they were handed over to Moses handed over by count, when they drew near to die He returned them by count — end quote. And this is what was intended at the beginning of the parashah <b>וַיֹּאמֶר HaShem אֶל מֹשֶׁה</b> etc., to say that even without the reason of ״אַחֲרֵי הַמַּגֵּפָה״ there is a reason in this counting in order to return them by count as stated, except that there was also another cause, which is ״אַחֲרֵי הַמַּגֵּפָה״, and so it turns out that two causes for the counting are alluded to in the midpoint of the beginning of the parashah in the middle of the verse."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:14:30.325385859Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}